WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:06.770
Using computer animation based on molecular research, we are now able to see how 

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.450
DNA is actually copied in living cells.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:16.530
You are looking at an assembly line of amazing miniature biochemical machines that are 

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:21.340
pulling apart the DNA double helix and cranking out a copy of each strand. The 

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:28.090
DNA to be copied enters the production line from bottom left.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.590
The whirling blue molecular machine is called helicase.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.780
It spins the DNA as fast as a jet engine as it unwinds the double helix 

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:40.080
into two strands.

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:46.680
One strand is copied continuously, and can be seen spooling off to the right.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:54.070
Things are not so simple for the other strand because it must be copied backwards.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.210
It is drawn out repeatedly in loops and copied one section at a time.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.890
The end result is two new DNA molecules. 

